{"reply":"You have two clean ways to use the ₱9,050.00 monthly surplus.

One leans toward Mara's goal: clear the credit card as fast as possible. The other keeps Jun's idea: put ₱4,000.00 extra on the card and send the rest to the emergency fund.

Because you want different things, I’m not picking one for you. If the main priority is getting rid of the card quickly, choose the faster-paydown option. If the main priority is reaching the emergency fund target while still paying the card down faster than the minimum, choose the split option.","options":[{"label":"A: all extra on the card","what_if":"debt1-W1","summary":"Put the full ₱9,050.00 monthly surplus on the credit card. That pays the card off in 9 months and results in ₱12,460.10 interest on that debt.","sources":["figure:debt1-W1:debt.payoff_months@debt:credit-card","figure:debt1-W1:debt.total_interest@debt:credit-card","fact:preference.debt_strategy","said"],"specialists":["debt"]},{"label":"B: ₱4,000 extra on the card, rest to emergency fund","what_if":"savings1-W1","summary":"Put ₱4,000.00 extra a month on the credit card and the rest to the emergency fund. The credit card is paid off in 17 months, and the emergency fund reaches its target in 12 months.","sources":["figure:savings1-W1:debt.payoff_months@debt:credit-card","figure:savings1-W1:savings.months_to_target","fact:account:emergency-fund/account.balance","said"],"specialists":["savings","debt"]}],"recommended":"","resolution":{"what_if":"","rejected":"","reason":"These two fit on their own, but they do different jobs. One clears the credit card in 9 months. The other clears it in 17 months and reaches the emergency fund target in 12 months. Mara asked to clear the card fast, while Jun wants part of the surplus to keep building cash, so this choice is yours."}}
